# =============================================================================
# Main Caddyfile — owned by root, AI cannot modify this file
# Location: /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#
# How this works:
#   Each site block maps a hostname to a local port where your app runs, and
#   Caddy gets a Let's Encrypt certificate for every block. Only add a block
#   for a hostname whose DNS points at this machine, with ports 80/443
#   reachable from the internet. For any other hostname Caddy retries the
#   certificate forever, logging errors and writing lock files as it goes.
#
# Using a Cloudflare Tunnel (step 15)?
#   Route each public hostname straight to its app's local port in the
#   Cloudflare dashboard, e.g. docket.YOUR_DOMAIN -> http://localhost:3002.
#   Cloudflare handles HTTPS, and this Caddy needs no site blocks at all.
#
# To add a project (no tunnel):
#   1. Add a block like:
#        myapp.YOUR_DOMAIN {
#            reverse_proxy localhost:PORT
#        }
#   2. Reload: sudo systemctl reload caddy
#
# To remove a project:
#   Delete its block and reload Caddy.
#
# The AI sandbox block (ai.YOUR_DOMAIN) forwards all AI traffic to port 4000,
# where the AI runs its own Caddy instance. The AI cannot modify THIS file —
# it can only control routing within its own sandbox. With a tunnel, route
# ai.YOUR_DOMAIN to http://localhost:4000 in the dashboard instead.
#
# SECURITY NOTES:
# • This Caddy's admin API (localhost:2019) is firewalled from the AI user
#   by the hardening script (step 4) — otherwise the AI could POST a new
#   config to it and hijack your routes without ever touching this file.
# • Port squatting: 'reverse_proxy localhost:3000' trusts whoever listens
#   on 3000. If your app is down, ANY local user (including the AI) could
#   bind that port and receive its traffic. For sensitive apps, prefer
#   unix sockets in a directory only your user can write to:
#       reverse_proxy unix//run/myapp/myapp.sock
# =============================================================================

# Example blocks, commented out. Uncomment and adapt only for hostnames that
# point at this machine (see "How this works" above).
#
# myapp.YOUR_DOMAIN {
#     reverse_proxy localhost:3000
# }
#
# AI sandbox: all traffic to ai.YOUR_DOMAIN goes to the AI's own Caddy on
# :4000, which routes it from there (see /srv/<ai-user>/Caddyfile).
# ai.YOUR_DOMAIN {
#     reverse_proxy localhost:4000
# }
